To explain Criteria API and Projections in Spring Boot, we will build a clean, production-ready project.

* Criteria API: Used for building dynamic, type-safe database queries using Java code instead of writing SQL strings.
* Projections: Used to fetch only specific columns (e.g., just firstName and email) instead of pulling entire heavy database rows,
which drastically improves application performance.

Here is the complete step-by-step guide using your Instructor and InstructorDetail domain.
------------------------------
## Step 1: Create the Project Structure
Use Spring Initializr (start.spring.io) or your IDE to generate a new project with these settings:

* Project: Maven
* Language: Java (17 or 21)
* Spring Boot: 3.x.x
* Dependencies: Spring Data JPA, Spring Web, MySQL Driver, Lombok

------------------------------
## Step 2: Configure Database (application.properties)
Update your properties file to connect to MySQL. We will use update for safety, and switch to a modern MySQL 8+ dialect.

spring.application.name=jpa-criteria-projection-demo

# Database Connection
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3006/instructor_db?useSSL=false&serverTimezone=UTC
spring.datasource.username=root
spring.datasource.password=yourpassword

# JPA / Hibernate Properties
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
spring.jpa.database-platform=org.hibernate.dialect.MySQLDialect
spring.jpa.show-sql=true
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.format_sql=true


* format_sql=true: Formats the printed SQL queries in your console so they are easy to read and debug.

------------------------------
## Step 3: Define the Entity Classes
We will use your Instructor and InstructorDetail entities. We add Lombok (@Getter, @Setter, @NoArgsConstructor)
to clean up boilerplate getters/setters.
## Instructor.java

package net.guides.springboot.jpa.model;
import jakarta.persistence.*;import lombok.Getter;import lombok.Setter;import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;import java.util.ArrayList;import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "instructor")
@Getter
@Setter
@NoArgsConstructor
public class Instructor {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(name = "first_name")
    private String firstName;

    @Column(name = "last_name")
    private String lastName;

    private String email;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "instructor", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<InstructorDetail> instructorDetails = new ArrayList<>();

    public Instructor(String firstName, String lastName, String email) {
        this.firstName = firstName;
        this.lastName = lastName;
        this.email = email;
    }

    // Helper method to synchronize bidirectional relationship
    public void addInstructorDetail(InstructorDetail detail) {
        instructorDetails.add(detail);
        detail.setInstructor(this);
    }
}

## InstructorDetail.java

package net.guides.springboot.jpa.model;
import jakarta.persistence.*;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonIgnore;
import lombok.Getter;
import lombok.Setter;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;

@Entity
@Table(name = "instructor_detail")
@Getter
@Setter
@NoArgsConstructor
public class InstructorDetail {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(name = "youtube_channel")
    private String youtubeChannel;

    private String hobby;

    @JsonIgnore
    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "instructor_id")
    private Instructor instructor;

    public InstructorDetail(String youtubeChannel, String hobby) {
        this.youtubeChannel = youtubeChannel;
        this.hobby = hobby;
    }
}


* jakarta.persistence.*: Spring Boot 3 uses Jakarta instead of javax.
* FetchType.LAZY: Prevents loading parent data unnecessarily when fetching details.

------------------------------
## Step 4: Create the Projection DTO
A Projection DTO (Data Transfer Object) is a custom Java class designed to catch only the specific fields we choose to select from the database.

package net.guides.springboot.jpa.dto;
import lombok.AllArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Getter;

@Getter
@AllArgsConstructor
public class InstructorSummaryDTO {
    private String firstName;
    private String email;
    private String hobby;
}


* @AllArgsConstructor: Essential. Hibernate uses this constructor to directly inject queried database fields into this object.

------------------------------
## Step 5: Implement Criteria & Projection Logic (Repository Layer)
Standard Spring Data JPA repositories use interfaces. To build a complex custom Criteria API query, we create a dedicated Custom Repository.
## Create the Interface: InstructorCustomRepository.java

package net.guides.springboot.jpa.repository;
import net.guides.springboot.jpa.dto.InstructorSummaryDTO;
import java.util.List;
public interface InstructorCustomRepository {
    List<InstructorSummaryDTO> findInstructorSummariesByHobby(String hobby);
}

## Create the Implementation: InstructorCustomRepositoryImpl.java
This is where the core logic of Criteria API and Projections happens.

package net.guides.springboot.jpa.repository;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import jakarta.persistence.PersistenceContext;
import jakarta.persistence.criteria.*;
import net.guides.springboot.jpa.dto.InstructorSummaryDTO;
import net.guides.springboot.jpa.model.Instructor;
import net.guides.springboot.jpa.model.InstructorDetail;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;
import java.util.List;

@Repository
public class InstructorCustomRepositoryImpl implements InstructorCustomRepository {

    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager entityManager;

    @Override
    public List<InstructorSummaryDTO> findInstructorSummariesByHobby(String hobby) {
        // 1. Initialize CriteriaBuilder and CriteriaQuery
        CriteriaBuilder cb = entityManager.getCriteriaBuilder();
        CriteriaQuery<InstructorSummaryDTO> query = cb.createQuery(InstructorSummaryDTO.class);

        // 2. Define the Root (The FROM clause)
        Root<Instructor> instructorRoot = query.from(Instructor.class);

        // 3. Perform a JOIN between Instructor and InstructorDetail tables
        Join<Instructor, InstructorDetail> detailJoin = instructorRoot.join("instructorDetails", JoinType.INNER);

        // 4. Implement Projection (Select ONLY specified fields into our DTO constructor)
        query.select(cb.construct(
                InstructorSummaryDTO.class,
                instructorRoot.get("firstName"),
                instructorRoot.get("email"),
                detailJoin.get("hobby")
        ));

        // 5. Add Where Conditions (The WHERE clause)
        Predicate hobbyPredicate = cb.equal(detailJoin.get("hobby"), hobby);
        query.where(hobbyPredicate);

        // 6. Execute the query using EntityManager
        return entityManager.createQuery(query).getResultList();
    }
}

## Step-by-Step Code Explanation:

   1. CriteriaBuilder cb: The factory engine used to construct query pieces (like equal, construct, join).
   2. CriteriaQuery<InstructorSummaryDTO>: Specifies that the final execution result of this query must map directly into our InstructorSummaryDTO wrapper rather than a database Entity.
   3. Root<Instructor>: Sets the main target table (FROM instructor).
   4. instructorRoot.join(...): Simulates an SQL INNER JOIN over to the instructor_detail table via the instructorDetails list variable name.
   5. cb.construct(...): This is the Projection mechanism. It binds specific selected table fields matching the order of the parameters declared inside the InstructorSummaryDTO constructor.
   6. cb.equal(...): Creates a dynamic filter condition dynamically translated down to WHERE hobby = '...'.

